\section{Reading the configuration}%
\label{prompt:config}

Both data directories can be relocated through nytid's
configuration, so we must read the same configuration file that the
CLI reads.
The CLI's configuration is handled by [[typerconf]], which derives
the location as
[[platformdirs.AppDirs(basename(sys.argv[0])).user_config_dir]]
plus [[config.json]].
That derivation is a trap for us: our [[sys.argv[0]]] is
[[prompt-nytid]], not [[nytid]], so calling [[typerconf]] directly
would look in the wrong directory.
We therefore hardcode the application name [[nytid]].

<<functions>>=
def config_file():
    """Return the path to nytid's configuration file.

    This is the same file `typerconf` reads for the `nytid`
    command: `AppDirs("nytid").user_config_dir` plus
    `config.json`.
    """
    dirs = platformdirs.AppDirs("nytid")
    return pathlib.Path(dirs.user_config_dir) / "config.json"
@

Let's verify that we derive the same path [[typerconf]] would
derive for the application name [[nytid]].
We reconstruct [[typerconf]]'s formula in the test rather than
importing [[typerconf]] itself, because under pytest
[[sys.argv[0]]] is [[pytest]], which would poison [[typerconf]]'s
module-level [[dirs]].

<<test functions>>=
def test_config_file_matches_typerconf():
    """`config_file()` equals the path typerconf derives."""
    expected = pathlib.Path(
        platformdirs.AppDirs("nytid").user_config_dir
    ) / "config.json"
    assert nytid.prompt.config_file() == expected
@

To look up a value we walk the dot-separated key through the JSON
structure, the same addressing scheme [[typerconf]] uses.
Being strictly read-only matters here: a prompt runs concurrently
with everything else and at every prompt render, so it must never
mutate shared state---and it does not need to, because it can simply
fall back to the same default value every time.
The CLI helpers [[get_todo_dir]] and [[get_tracking_storage]] once
differed on exactly this point, persisting the default back into the
configuration on a missing key; they now read through the cached,
write-free [[default]] helper (\cref{cli.utils.defaults.cache}), so
both sides of the duplication agree on the semantics the
drift-guards pin down.
Any error at all---missing file, malformed JSON, missing
key---yields the fallback, because for a prompt a default answer is
always better than a traceback.

<<functions>>=
def config_value(key, fallback):
    """Return the config value at dot-separated `key`.

    Returns `fallback` on any error (missing file, malformed
    JSON, missing key).  Never writes the configuration.
    """
    try:
        with open(config_file()) as conf:
            data = json.load(conf)
        for part in key.split("."):
            data = data[part]
        return data
    except (OSError, ValueError, KeyError, TypeError):
        return fallback
@

Let's verify both the found and the fallback case, and that a
corrupt configuration file degrades to the fallback.

