\section{Active todo ids}

The todo state lives in two files in the todo directory
(\cref{cli.todo}): [[todos.json]] holds
[[{"next_id": ..., "todos": [...]}]] and [[active_stack.json]]
holds a plain JSON list of todo ids, most recently started last.
The stack on disk may be stale---it can reference ids that were
deleted, ids whose todo has since been marked done, or contain
duplicates---so the real reader, [[load_todo_state]], filters it
through [[normalize_active_stack]] (the
[[<<active stack helpers>>]] in \cref{cli.todo}): keep the stack
order, drop ids missing from the todos, ids whose status is
[[done]], and duplicates.
We replicate exactly that filter; anything less and the prompt
would show todos the CLI no longer considers active.
The reader returns the parent map alongside the stack because the
window chain (\cref{prompt:chain}) needs both, and one parse of
[[todos.json]] per prompt is the whole budget for that file.

<<functions>>=
