\section{Active tracking labels}

The track module stores each worker's current session as JSON in
the tracking directory (\cref{cli.track}).
Three details of the real reader matter to us.
First, the filename embeds the worker name, sanitized for
filesystem use by [[sanitize_who]]: lowercase, keep alphanumerics
and hyphens, replace everything else with underscores.

<<functions>>=
def sanitize_who(who):
    """Sanitize a worker name for use in filenames.

    Replicates `sanitize_who` in `nytid.cli.track`: lowercase,
    keep alphanumerics and hyphens, everything else becomes an
    underscore.
    """
    return "".join(
        c if c.isalnum() or c == "-" else "_"
        for c in who.lower()
    )
@

Second, the file rule of [[get_current_session_file]]: the session
lives in [[current_session_<sanitized>.json]], but when [[who]] is
the default user (the [[USER]] environment variable) and the
suffixed file does not exist, the reader falls back to the legacy
unsuffixed [[current_session.json]] from before sessions became
per-worker.
We replicate the fallback because a user who has not tracked since
that migration would otherwise see an empty prompt while
[[nytid track status]] still shows active labels.
The unset-[[USER]] case used to be a deliberate divergence: the
track module fell back to the worker name [[me]] while we used the
empty string, on the argument that a prompt only ever runs in an
interactive shell.  That argument holds, but the divergence bought
nothing and cost a reader of both files the question of which
fallback is the real one, so we now spell the same [[me]] the CLI
does (\cref{cli:default-username}).

<<functions>>=
def session_file(who):
    """Return the session file for worker `who`.

    Replicates `get_current_session_file` in `nytid.cli.track`,
    including the fallback to the legacy unsuffixed
    `current_session.json` for the default user.
    """
    directory = tracking_dir()
    suffixed = directory / (
        f"current_session_{sanitize_who(who)}.json"
    )
    if who == os.environ.get("USER", DEFAULT_WHO):
        if not suffixed.exists():
            legacy = directory / "current_session.json"
            if legacy.exists():
                return legacy
    return suffixed
<<constants>>=
DEFAULT_WHO = "me"
@

The drift guard for the fallback cannot import
[[nytid.cli.get_default_username]] the way the other guards import
[[nytid.cli.track]]: the guards run under pytest, which has already
paid the import cost, but the value has to match a module that this
one must never import at run time.  We therefore compare against the
real helper in the test only.

