\section{Staying cheap}

The whole point of the module is the import bill, so we guard it
with a test: a fresh interpreter that imports [[nytid.prompt]]
must not have loaded any of the heavyweight modules the CLI drags
in, nor [[nytid.cli]] itself.
A subprocess is essential here---inside the test process pytest
and the drift guards have already imported everything, so
[[sys.modules]] proves nothing.

<<test functions>>=
def test_import_is_cheap():
    """Importing `nytid.prompt` avoids the CLI import tax."""
    src_dir = pathlib.Path(
        nytid.prompt.__file__
    ).parent.parent
    probe = (
        "import sys, nytid.prompt;"
        "print('\\n'.join(sorted(sys.modules)))"
    )
    env = dict(os.environ, PYTHONPATH=str(src_dir))
    result = subprocess.run(
        [sys.executable, "-c", probe],
        capture_output=True, text=True, env=env, check=True,
    )
    loaded = set(result.stdout.split())
    forbidden = {
        "typer", "click", "rich", "icalendar",
        "requests", "nytid.cli",
    }
    assert not loaded & forbidden
@

